The 400 is likely from bean validation: your numeric fields are `int` (primitive) so missing JSON fields deserialize to `0`, and `@Positive` rejects `0`. Change to wrapper `Integer` and add `@NotNull` so missing values are reported clearly. Also ensure your request has `Content-Type: application/json` and uses the field names `name`, `calories`, `carbohydrates`, `protein`, `fat`.

Updated `backend/src/main/java/dev/jclawlor/FitnessGoals/dto/MealDto.java`:

```java
java
package dev.jclawlor.FitnessGoals.dto;

import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotEmpty;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotNull;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Positive;

import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Data;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;

@Data
@NoArgsConstructor
@AllArgsConstructor
public class MealDto {

	private Long id;

	@NotEmpty
	private String name;

	@NotNull
	@Positive
	private Integer calories;

	@NotNull
	@Positive
	private Integer carbohydrates;

	@NotNull
	@Positive
	private Integer protein;

	@NotNull
	@Positive
	private Integer fat;
}
```

Replace `@NotEmpty` with `@NotBlank` so null and whitespace-only names fail validation. Also ensure the controller parameter is annotated with `@Valid` and `@RequestBody`.

